WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.450
YUFENG GUO: I know that you
use debuggers the right way

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.820
every time, and would
never use print statements

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.400
to debug your code, right?

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.180
Because if you did, you might
find that TensorFlow's print

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.610
statement doesn't quite work
the same way as typical print

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:15.540
statements.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.060
Today I'll show you how
TensorFlow's print statements

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.550
work and how to make
the most of them,

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.790
hopefully saving you
some time along the way.

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:27.270
There are a couple ways
to get things to print out

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.070
while writing TensorFlow code.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.299
Of course, there's the classic
built-in Python function,

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:33.420
print.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.270
Then there is TensorFlow's
print function,

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:39.150
tf.Print with a capital P.
When working with TensorFlow,

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:42.180
it's important to remember
that everything is ultimately

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:43.930
a graph computation.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:46.740
This means that if you just use
Python to print a TensorFlow

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.460
operation, it will simply
show a description of what

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.950
that operation is
since no values have

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:54.560
been passed through it yet.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.790
It will also often
show the dimensions

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:00.430
that are expected to be in
that node if they're known.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:01.990
If you want to print
the values that

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:05.110
are flowing through a
particular part of the graph

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:07.480
as it's being
executed, then we'll

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.070
need to turn to using tf.Print.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:13.990
Often we think of
print as something

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:17.440
that we add on to the side of
something, just let it run,

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.440
and then come back to our
normal flow of operations.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.230
In TensorFlow, only
the nodes of the graph

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:26.720
that need to be executed
will be executed.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.550
So if you have a dangling
print node in your graph,

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:32.330
it won't be executed.

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:34.030
So what are we to do?

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:38.890
We need to splice the print
call into the graph like so.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.200
The way this
manifests in code is

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.080
to have the print call
take as its first parameter

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.690
the node that is
its input, and then

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:49.150
assign the output
to a variable that

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:52.480
will serve as an input in
a later node in the graph,

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.210
thus linking the print
statement serially

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:58.270
into the flow of operations.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:00.520
While the print statement
does no computation

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.710
and just passes
the value onward,

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:06.140
it will print the desired
node as a side effect.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.139
Another behavior that's
a bit unlike what

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:10.090
we're used to seeing
in a print statement

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.580
is that the print
node that we introduce

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.880
is merely locking in when the
print statement will happen,

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:19.190
namely when the node is reached
in the computational graph.

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:21.730
However, there aren't
really many restrictions

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:23.530
on what you print.

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:26.530
That is, you can print just
about any node in the graph

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:27.850
that you can access.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.920
This is the second argument
to the tf.Print call--

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.670
an array of nodes to print.

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:35.710
Often we'll just
use the same node

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:38.140
as the first argument,
which was that input,

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.050
but we could include a lot more
nodes in the print statement

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:42.940
if we'd like.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.660
There is also a third argument--

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:46.980
a message.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:50.180
This will allow you to prepend
some string before printing out

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.940
those nodes so you can
easily find a given print

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.620
statement in your logs.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:56.930
So there you have it--

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:59.450
tf.Print in a nutshell.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:02.930
But where might you utilize
tf.Print in your code?

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.750
I often find myself using
tf.Print in my input functions

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:13.350
to debug exactly what's being
passed into my training.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:16.710
Merely using the Python print
is not quite enough here

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.640
since it will only print once--

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.600
when the input function
graph is constructed.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.780
So instead, we'll
introduce the tf.Print call

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.930
into the input
function's data pipeline.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:30.510
Notice that we are just
printing the same value

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:33.690
that we passed in as the
input, but you could certainly

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:37.110
print other nodes in
this array as well.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:38.880
A word of caution here--

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.540
if you use tf.Print in
your input function,

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:44.100
be sure to set
some kind of limits

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.890
around the amount of data
that you're passing in.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:52.050
Otherwise, you may end up
with a really long log file.

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:55.020
There are many ways in which
TensorFlow's print statement is

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.060
not your typical
print statement,

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.340
but it can be used
to great effect

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:03.810
when you need to see the
tensors flowing in your graph.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:07.530
Thanks for watching this
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.450
Be sure to subscribe
to the channel

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:12.840
to catch future episodes
right when they come out.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:16.440
And for now, go see how
TensorFlow's print statements

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.500
can help you get more
clarity into how your machine

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.000
learning is running.

